<Version>
"32101", "1"
</Version>

<! ============================================================
<! CCSTA TEST IMPORT -- built 2026-08-05 from Sage's published
<! .IMP specification, not from an export.
<!
<! *** IMPORT THIS INTO A BACKUP COPY OF YOUR COMPANY FILE. ***
<! Not the live books. If it works it creates a real invoice,
<! and undoing that is more annoying than making a backup.
<!
<! Lines starting with <! are comments. Sage ignores them, so
<! this whole block is safe to leave in.
<! ============================================================

<! -- THINGS YOU MAY NEED TO CHANGE ---------------------------
<!
<! 1. VERSION, first line above. "32101" is Sage 50 2025.
<!    If yours is older this is the most likely thing to fail.
<!    Help > About in Sage tells you the version.
<!
<! 2. CUSTOMER NAME, the line below. It must match a customer
<!    that ALREADY EXISTS in Sage, character for character.
<!    If it doesn't, Sage stops and asks you to pick one --
<!    which is not a failure, just a prompt.
<!
<! 3. ITEM CODES "CHARTER" and "FUEL" further down. If your
<!    Sage file has no such items, Sage will ask you to pick
<!    an account for each line. Replace them with whatever
<!    codes you actually use, or leave them and answer the
<!    prompts to see what it wants.
<! -----------------------------------------------------------

<SalInvoice>
<! Customer line: name, street, city, province, postal code
"REPLACE WITH A REAL SAGE CUSTOMER", "123 Example St", "Surrey", "British Columbia", "V4N 3G2"
<! Options line: no. of detail lines, invoice no., date, payment
<! method code, payment method, invoice total, amount paid
<! NOTE: the payment-method fields are the part I'm least sure
<! of -- Sage documents them in an image I couldn't read, so
<! "1"/"Pay Later" is inferred. If the import complains, this
<! line is the first place to look.
"2", "TEST-001", "08-05-2026", "1", "Pay Later", "538.13", "0.00"
<! Detail lines: item code, quantity, unit price, amount,
<! then per-tax-authority: name, exempt flag, taxable flag,
<! rate, tax amount
"CHARTER", "5.0000", "92.5000", "462.50", "GST", "0", "1", "5.00", "23.13"
"FUEL", "1.0000", "50.0000", "50.00", "GST", "0", "1", "5.00", "2.50"
</SalInvoice>

<! -- WHAT THIS INVOICE SAYS ----------------------------------
<! A typical CCSTA charter, using your real rate config:
<!
<!   5 hours x $92.50/hr (47-bench, non-member)   $462.50
<!   Fuel surcharge                                $50.00
<!   ---------------------------------------------------
<!   Subtotal                                     $512.50
<!   GST 5%                                        $25.63
<!   ---------------------------------------------------
<!   Total                                        $538.13
<!
<! Only ONE tax authority (GST). The Sage sample shows GST and
<! PST on every line; your surcharge_config has gst_rate_pct
<! and no PST, so PST is omitted here. If Sage rejects the line
<! for having too few fields, that's the reason and it's an
<! easy fix.
<! -----------------------------------------------------------

<! -- WHAT WE LEARN ------------------------------------------
<! It imports cleanly       -> the whole approach works, and
<!                             generating these per invoice is
<!                             straightforward.
<! It complains about a
<! specific field           -> even better. That names the exact
<!                             problem, which is what the last
<!                             attempt never produced.
<! It refuses the file
<! outright                 -> likely the version number, or the
<!                             edition doesn't support import.
<!
<! Any of the three is progress. The only bad outcome is not
<! trying it.
<! -----------------------------------------------------------
